"""Consistent one- and two-lump TEC control simulation for Module 6.

The program uses the same dimensional variables and controller definitions as
``Lab_6_pi_contribution_rolling_demo.py``.

One-lump process:

    C*dT/dt = P_u(u)*u - H*(T - T_amb),    C = C_T + C_m

Two-lump process:

    C_T*dT/dt   = P_u(u)*u - G*(T - T_m)
    C_m*dT_m/dt = G*(T - T_m) - H*(T_m - T_amb)

The controller always uses the measured temperature: T for the one-lump model
and T_m for the two-lump model.

The TEC coefficient is piecewise: P_u = P_u,h for heating and P_u = P_u,c for
cooling.  Their ratio r = P_u,h/P_u,c = chi_h/chi_c defaults to 2.

    open loop: u = u_user
    P:         u = Kp*e
    PI:        u = Kp*e + Ki*integral(e dt)
    feedforward: u = u_ff(T_set), the inverse of the steady-state curve
    e = T_set - T_measured

Hardware-only raw diagnostics are ``raw_sine`` and ``raw_offset_sine``.
The offset is initialized from the measured steady-state curve at 40 C and
must remain greater than its amplitude so the TEC stays on the heating branch.

Run the desktop GUI from this repository root:

    python ClaudeTest

Run a non-interactive two-lump demonstration and save a PNG:

    python ClaudeTest --demo

The GUI can run the mathematical model or connect to the Module 7 Arduino
controller over a 9600-baud serial connection. For hardware control, select
"Arduino hardware", choose the board's serial port, connect, then resume.
Start connected and paused with ``python ClaudeTest --hardware --port COM3``.
Hardware feedforward and offset-sine modes use the model-line susceptibility
by default; a measured steady-state CSV can optionally replace it.
"""

